dy  of research  um prior research things like reduced  family income  um food insecurities stress  in families among parents um raging all  the way to potential  child abuse and spousal abuse that  children might experience  lack of access to outdoor spaces all of  these things can leave a biological  residue  and really these massive shocks can get  under the skin of our children and stick  with us and there's a there's a whole  range of really interesting biology uh  candice you alluded to  um things like the immune system brain  development  all of these physiological processes can  be affected  by these by these social environments so  that's really sort of at the heart of  where this proposal uh  is rooted um and the idea then is  can we now measure directly in  children that are exposed to these  current pandemic conditions  um whether these predictions that we  know from from this large body of  research that's  really become way before covet um and  that's where for example the work that  we had proposed in the manual life  sifa uh proposal that also then  intersects with what megan of course is  doing on the on the canadian  child card yeah so this is interesting  most discussions about children have  really focused on school openings and  not bio  biology or long-term consequences in  other aspects so this will be important  um megan um you are involved in two  pretty large scale studies right now the  the child study the code of child study  and also a study that's looking at  pre-term birth during the pandemic so  what are some of the kind of questions  you're coming into this with and how  does it frame kind of how you think  about working with the larger team  on these issues yeah so mike mentioned  the child study and  the importance of looking at long-term  impacts and so the child study is a  national canadian birth cohort study  it's actually been ongoing for more than  a decade so we enrolled 3 500 families  with  children born between 2010 and 2