th  research to launch this study  where we'll be looking both at the  direct impact so if some of the children  or parents in the study do become  infected and develop covid  we'll be able to capture that and ask  questions about you know who got the  most sick  um who did not get sick and had no  symptoms and is there something about  their biology which we know a lot about  from the study  or their um kind of social environment  at home that able  enabled them to be resilient and then  the indirect impact which  you know hopefully not too many children  will contract ovid  but we know all of them are living  through this period of transition and  stress and we'll be able to look at that  in the study  the second project i'm involved in as  you mentioned is looking at pre-term  birth  so this is an idea that blossomed in the  last two months and has really exploded  into a large international study  where we're focusing on the the period  of pregnancy  and looking at whether the experience of  the pandemic again  um is affecting perinatal outcomes so  rates of preterm birth and stillbirth  and how that might be different in  settings like canada versus  low and middle income countries so  that's been a really exciting  new project as well that's incredible  and i'm just thinking about those two  great resources will allow  in canada to track the long-term impacts  as you follow these children over years  and decades  one of the things that we've been  wondering a lot about the child brain  development program  is the impact that this has had on  research the pandemic itself  and how we can continue to collect this  type of valuable data  from children and from families when we  have to remain apart and so mike i know  that your group has been working on some  of this kind of innovation around the  measures can you say a bit about  how your ponies to address this yeah  that's uh that's an excellent point and  making alluded to this that you know  during the pandemi