ct  together and figure out how to collect  these samples from home  and that's going to really open up new  opportunities as research  carries on after the pandemic i think in  terms of differential risk  that is something we anticipate seeing  um and because we have such a large  study  covering four different provinces and  you know different strata  socioeconomic status we do anticipate  that  the associations we see won't be the  same for every population group  and so it could be that you know  families who  maybe have had to deal with more stress  earlier in the child's lifetime will be  more resilient to  tackling this pandemic or it could be  that having those stressful experiences  earlier on  have really embedded a more susceptible  profile so those are things that we'll  be able to tackle  in the pre-term birth study a really  interesting example of this is  we really got interested in the study  because there have been some  um reports um in the media  saying that in places like canada the uk  denmark there has actually been a  dramatic reduction in preterm births  during the pandemic um which is  surprising to people because  um that would seem to be a good thing  you have to think a little bit more  about  about it first of all is it a good thing  is there for example more stillbirths  that are  um counteracting that but also once we  started talking to low and middle income  countries they said well that's really  not what we're seeing  um we're seeing an increase and we're  seeing more still bursts and so  really understanding that the pandemic  and the associated lockdowns  and control measures might be affecting  different countries or different  populations within countries in very  different ways  that's right i'm going to remind  everyone that we're approaching kind of  the halfway mark of discussion and so  for those of you in the audience if you  have questions i invite you to kind of  drop them into the  into the q a section now um  and one 